\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Doppler peak and local noise floor for each target speed (capture~1 of each speed, Welch PSD with $\Delta f = \SI {10}{kHz}$). $f_D$ is the power-weighted centroid of the excess PSD over the stationary-target reference, and $v_{\mathrm  {LOS}} = f_D / (\SI {1.29}{MHz.s/m})$. The PSD at $f_D$ is that of the bin nearest the centroid; the noise statistics are taken in a band outside $3\times $FWHM around the centroid, out to \SI {2}{MHz} either side. All PSD values are in the same units, so the PSD at $f_D$ can be compared directly with the detection threshold (mean $+\,5\sigma $), which it exceeds at every speed. The excess power is the PSD above the local noise, integrated across the peak.}}{43}{table.5.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:doppler_vs_rpm}{{5.1}{43}{Doppler peak and local noise floor for each target speed (capture~1 of each speed, Welch PSD with $\Delta f = \SI {10}{kHz}$). $f_D$ is the power-weighted centroid of the excess PSD over the stationary-target reference, and $v_{\mathrm {LOS}} = f_D / (\SI {1.29}{MHz.s/m})$. The PSD at $f_D$ is that of the bin nearest the centroid; the noise statistics are taken in a band outside $3\times $FWHM around the centroid, out to \SI {2}{MHz} either side. All PSD values are in the same units, so the PSD at $f_D$ can be compared directly with the detection threshold (mean $+\,5\sigma $), which it exceeds at every speed. The excess power is the PSD above the local noise, integrated across the peak}{table.5.1}{}}
